WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:08.023
How can nuclei that never reach the spinal cord decide whether a movement goes

2
00:00:08.023 --> 00:00:09.023
ahead?

3
00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:11.727
Start with a picture: a column of boxes.

4
00:00:12.587 --> 00:00:15.027
The cortex is at the top, then an input stage.

5
00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:21.230
An output stage feeds the thalamus, which sends an arrow back to the cortex, closing

6
00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:22.230
a loop.

7
00:00:22.583 --> 00:00:26.243
Now the names. The input stage is the striatum.

8
00:00:27.103 --> 00:00:30.603
The output stage is the internal globus pallidus, GPi.

9
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:36.090
Its output neurons are inhibitory and fire steadily, even at rest.

10
00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:39.630
So the thalamus is held down, like a foot on a brake.

11
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:43.633
In the classic model, movement needs the brake lifted.

12
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:47.167
Two routes run from the striatum to the brake.

13
00:00:48.247 --> 00:00:50.807
Striatal projection neurons are inhibitory too.

14
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.520
In the direct route, striatal neurons with D1 receptors inhibit the brake.

15
00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:58.860
The brake inhibits the thalamus.

16
00:00:59.817 --> 00:01:01.817
Two inhibitions, an even number.

17
00:01:02.677 --> 00:01:06.037
More striatal firing releases the thalamus, toward movement.

18
00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:09.010
The indirect route takes a detour.

19
00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:15.090
Striatal neurons with D2 receptors inhibit the external globus pallidus, GPe.

20
00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:19.727
GPe inhibits the subthalamic nucleus, STN.

21
00:01:20.287 --> 00:01:22.287
And the STN excites the brake.

22
00:01:23.267 --> 00:01:30.087
Walk it through. D2 neurons fire more, so GPe fires less, and the freed STN fires

23
00:01:30.087 --> 00:01:31.087
more.

24
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.280
The brake, excited, fires more.

25
00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:35.720
The thalamus is held down harder.

26
00:01:36.783 --> 00:01:38.723
Three inhibitions, an odd number.

27
00:01:39.563 --> 00:01:41.183
This route pushes away from movement.

28
00:01:42.217 --> 00:01:46.117
Now add dopamine, from the substantia nigra pars compacta.

29
00:01:46.797 --> 00:01:48.697
It acts on the first box of each route.

30
00:01:49.577 --> 00:01:51.157
Treat each action as one more link.

31
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:57.620
In the classic model, dopamine excites the D1 neurons and inhibits the D2 neurons.

32
00:01:58.683 --> 00:02:03.163
Acting through each receptor, does dopamine push toward movement or away from it?

33
00:02:09.083 --> 00:02:10.763
Toward movement, both ways.

34
00:02:11.683 --> 00:02:13.623
Through D1, dopamine adds no flip.

35
00:02:14.583 --> 00:02:18.503
Through D2, its own inhibition adds one flip to the route's three.

36
00:02:19.343 --> 00:02:20.643
Four flips, an even number.

37
00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:23.083
The two actions agree.

38
00:02:24.063 --> 00:02:27.043
So losing dopamine pushes both routes toward less movement.

39
00:02:27.823 --> 00:02:30.323
The direct route weakens, the indirect strengthens.

40
00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:36.317
So the brake fires more for two reasons: less inhibition from the striatum, more

41
00:02:36.317 --> 00:02:37.897
excitation from the STN.

42
00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:43.740
The thalamus is held down harder, and movement is hard to start: the model's

43
00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.020
prediction of akinesia.

44
00:02:45.983 --> 00:02:47.603
The model rests on a measurement.

45
00:02:48.523 --> 00:02:54.043
In 1960, post-mortem brains from people with Parkinson's disease showed striatal

46
00:02:54.043 --> 00:02:55.423
dopamine greatly reduced.

47
00:02:56.517 --> 00:02:59.297
Inhibit an inhibitor, and you release its target.
